=======================================================================
 olele -- commands you run from the repo root
=======================================================================

Every *_all.sh script and pc_app.sh acts on ONE variant, chosen by the
VARIANT env var. It DEFAULTS TO "sizif", so marathon work needs it set
every time -- either per command:

    VARIANT=marathon ./run_all.sh

or once per shell:

    source ./bootenv.sh                # also puts the Xilinx tools on PATH
    export VARIANT=marathon

VARIANT must be an env var, never a positional argument: pc_app.sh passes
all its arguments straight through, so "./pc_app.sh marathon start" would
hand "marathon" to system.sh as a subcommand and fail.

    sizif     AXI-Lite architecture, frozen reference
    marathon  DMA / AXI-Stream architecture, active development

Only one variant can own the board at a time -- they flash the same part.
Both also use TCP port 5001, so stop one before starting the other.


-----------------------------------------------------------------------
 EVERY DAY
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

  source ./bootenv.sh              Once per shell, before anything else.
                                   SOURCE it, do not execute it.

  ./net_tune.sh                    Once per BOOT, before any throughput
                                   work. Kernel state, so a reboot loses
                                   it and the stream goes unstable again.

  VARIANT=marathon ./run_all.sh    Flash + run the board, and start the
                                   PC app. Needs build_all.sh done first.

  VARIANT=marathon ./stop_all.sh   Stop the PC app and the serial console.
                                   The firmware keeps running until reset,
                                   reflashed or power-cycled.


-----------------------------------------------------------------------
 BUILDING
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

  VARIANT=marathon ./build_all.sh    Whole stack: Vivado bitstream + .xsa,
                                     Vitis platform + firmware ELF, relay
                                     binary + venv. Slow. Builds only, does
                                     not flash or start anything.

  VARIANT=marathon ./compile_all.sh  Fast path: firmware C and the C++ relay
                                     only. Use after editing app/src/*.c or
                                     tcp_server_app.cpp. Skips Vivado synth
                                     and Vitis platform creation.

  VARIANT=marathon ./clean_all.sh    Wipe that variant's build/ dirs. The
                                     other variant is left alone so a slow
                                     Vivado rebuild is not triggered by
                                     accident.

  Rule of thumb: build_all.sh only when the bitstream or the Vitis platform
  itself changed. Otherwise compile_all.sh. Neither is needed for a
  Python-only change -- pc_app.sh restart-client covers that.


-----------------------------------------------------------------------
 PC SIDE ONLY  (no reflash -- passthrough to pc_app/$VARIANT/system.sh)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

  VARIANT=marathon ./pc_app.sh start
                              stop
                              restart           both relay and client
                              restart-client    after editing config.py,
                                                net.py, plot.py, ...
                              restart-server    after editing
                                                tcp_server_app.cpp
                              status            what is running
                              logs              tail both logs

  restart-client is the one to prefer while experimenting: it leaves the
  relay up, so the board's TCP connection survives and the board-side [S]
  stats stay continuous.

  Panel fields (send rate, chunk size, frame rate, trigger, heart rate...)
  are live -- they need NO restart at all.


-----------------------------------------------------------------------
 NETWORK TUNING / DIAGNOSIS
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

  ./net_tune.sh          Apply. Needs sudo. Existing TCP connections do
                         NOT pick it up -- restart the PC side after.
  ./net_tune.sh show     Is it applied? No sudo needed. FIRST THING TO
                         CHECK if throughput is fluctuating.
  ./net_tune.sh loss     Live packet loss to the board. ~0.85% is the
                         known standing figure; rising means you are
                         pushing past what the board's rx path absorbs.
  ./net_tune.sh revert   Remove the flags.


-----------------------------------------------------------------------
 WHEN SOMETHING LOOKS WRONG -- CHECK IN THIS ORDER
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

  1. ./net_tune.sh show            untuned after a reboot is the most
                                   likely cause of "it fluctuates"
  2. VARIANT=... ./pc_app.sh logs  the client line tells you WHERE:
                                     (dropped N late)  -> local PC stall
                                     (N send-stalls)   -> far end pushing
                                                          back
                                   both near zero and still short of the
                                   setpoint -> the limit is elsewhere
  3. the board's PuTTY console     [E] lines, and the [S] rate; tx= shows
                                   only when it differs from rx, and +/-1
                                   is just a window-boundary straddle
  4. ./net_tune.sh loss            link health

  Note the board has been the innocent party in every problem so far.
  Suspect the PC and the link first.
